pages.T09 = render('T09', {
  description: 'Build nested AND/OR rule groups, such as Engineering AND (Blocked OR due this week), and publish them as a Confluence table’s starting view.',
  lede: 'Column filters answer “this and that.” Some questions need “this and (that or the other).” Advanced groups let you write that expression directly, nest it, and publish it as the page’s starting view.',
  result: `<p><strong>What you will end with:</strong> Engineering AND (Blocked OR due this week) returning 5 of 24 tasks, a nested version that adds an estimate threshold and returns 4, and the expression published as the table’s default.</p>`,
  body: `<h2>Why separate column filters fall short</h2><p>In the column filters, several values chosen for one column match any of them, and filters on different columns must all match. Choosing Team Engineering, Status Blocked and Due <strong>This week</strong> therefore asks for Engineering tasks that are blocked <em>and</em> due this week. In the sample tracker, none are. The question is really Engineering AND (Blocked OR due this week).</p>${sampleBlock('project-tracker.csv', 'Sample tracker', { preview: 4 })}${todayNote}
<h2>How advanced groups combine</h2><ul><li><strong>Match groups</strong> joins the top-level groups: <strong>All groups (AND)</strong> or <strong>Any group (OR)</strong>.</li><li>Each group has its own <strong>Match rules in group</strong> setting: <strong>All rules (AND)</strong> or <strong>Any rule (OR)</strong>. Nested groups keep their own choice too.</li><li>Search and column filters still apply alongside the advanced groups; all must match.</li></ul>
<h2>Step 1: Build Engineering AND (Blocked OR due this week)</h2><ol><li>Choose <strong>Filters</strong>, then <strong>Advanced groups</strong>. Keep <strong>Match groups</strong> as <strong>All groups (AND)</strong>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Add group</strong>. Set <strong>Rule column</strong> to Team, <strong>Rule condition</strong> to Equals and <strong>Rule value</strong> to <code>Engineering</code>. Choose <strong>Save rule</strong>. This is group 1.</li><li>Choose <strong>Add group</strong> again. Create Status, Equals, <code>Blocked</code> and save it. This is group 2.</li><li>Set <strong>Match rules in group 2</strong> to <strong>Any rule (OR)</strong>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Add rule to group 2</strong>. Set <strong>Rule column</strong> to Due, <strong>Rule condition</strong> to <strong>Relative date</strong> and <strong>Rule date preset</strong> to <strong>This week</strong>. Save the rule.</li></ol><p>The panel shows each group with its match setting and numbered rules. In outline:</p>${code(`Group 1   Match rules in group 1: All rules (AND)
  1.1. Team equals Engineering (Text)
Group 2   Match rules in group 2: Any rule (OR)
  2.1. Status equals Blocked (Text)
  2.2. Due: This week (Date, YYYY-MM-DD)`)}<p>Choose <strong>Done</strong>. The count line reads <strong>5 of 24 rows</strong>; sorted by Due:</p>${rowsFrom('project-tracker.csv', ex.T09.twoGroups, ['Task ID', 'Task', 'Owner', 'Status', 'Due', 'Estimate'], { caption: 'Expected result: 5 of 24 rows, Due ascending', numeric: ['Estimate'] })}<p>Switch <strong>Match groups</strong> to <strong>Any group (OR)</strong> to see the difference: every Engineering task plus anything blocked or due this week, ${ex.T09.anyGroupCount} rows in all. Switch back before continuing.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Nest a third level</h2><p>Suppose the meeting only cares about this week’s work if it is estimated at three days or more: Engineering AND (Blocked OR (due this week AND Estimate at least 3)).</p><ol><li>Choose <strong>Remove rule 2.2</strong> to remove the plain due-this-week rule.</li><li>Choose <strong>Add nested group to group 2</strong>. Create Due, <strong>Relative date</strong>, <strong>This week</strong> and save it. The nested group is group 2.2 and starts as <strong>All rules (AND)</strong>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Add rule to group 2.2</strong>. Set <strong>Rule column</strong> to Estimate, keep <strong>Rule type</strong> as Number, set <strong>Rule condition</strong> to <strong>At least</strong> and <strong>Rule value</strong> to <code>3</code>. Save the rule.</li></ol>${rowsFrom('project-tracker.csv', ex.T09.nested, ['Task ID', 'Task', 'Status', 'Due', 'Estimate'], { caption: 'Expected result: 4 of 24 rows', numeric: ['Estimate'] })}<p>PRJ-116 drops out: it is due this week but estimated at 2 days. The blocked tasks stay regardless of estimate.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Publish it as the starting view</h2><ol><li>Choose <strong>Copy advanced filter settings</strong>. Copy everything in <strong>Advanced filter settings</strong>.</li><li>Edit the page, open the macro settings, choose <strong>Column filters and advanced rules</strong> and paste into <strong>Default advanced filter groups</strong>.</li><li><strong>Starting view preview</strong> counts the matches. For the Step 1 expression it reports <strong>5 of 24 rows match</strong>. Choose <strong>Save settings</strong> and publish.</li></ol><p>Only the applied advanced groups are copied; search, column filters, sorting and layout are separate settings. The copied text names columns by header and stores relative presets, not dates:</p><details><summary>Show the copied settings for the Step 1 expression</summary>${code(`{
  "version": 1,
  "match": "all",
  "groups": [
    {
      "match": "all",
      "rules": [
        {
          "header": "Team",
          "condition": {
            "type": "text",
            "dateFormat": "iso",
            "operator": "equals",
            "value": "Engineering",
            "valueTo": "",
            "preset": ""
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "match": "any",
      "rules": [
        {
          "header": "Status",
          "condition": {
            "type": "text",
            "dateFormat": "iso",
            "operator": "equals",
            "value": "Blocked",
            "valueTo": "",
            "preset": ""
          }
        },
        {
          "header": "Due",
          "condition": {
            "type": "date",
            "dateFormat": "iso",
            "operator": "relative",
            "value": "",
            "valueTo": "",
            "preset": "thisWeek"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}`)}</details><p>If a header named in the settings is later renamed, readers see <strong>Check the table's default view</strong>, and the advanced default is skipped rather than partly applied. Build the rule again and copy fresh settings.</p>
<h2>Limits and behavior</h2><ul><li>Up to 5 top-level groups and 5 rules or nested groups in each group, nested up to four levels, with at most 100 groups and rules in total.</li><li>A draft applies only after <strong>Save rule</strong>. If you leave the panel with a draft open, the toolbar shows <strong>An advanced rule has unsaved changes.</strong> with <strong>Continue editing rule</strong>.</li><li>Each rule keeps its own type and format. A number rule has <strong>Rule number format</strong>; a date rule has <strong>Rule date format</strong>.</li><li>If any rule is invalid, the warning <strong>Check your advanced filters</strong> names it, and the whole expression matches no rows until you fix it. It never applies half an expression.</li></ul>
${related(['T01', 'T02', 'T06', 'T10'])}`,
});

