pages.T10 = render('T10', {
  description: 'Set explicit date and number formats in Flint Table Filter so day-first dates, comma decimals, blanks and invalid cells filter, sort and total correctly.',
  lede: '09/11/2026 is 9 November in one office and 11 September in another. 1.250 is one and a quarter in one locale and twelve hundred fifty in another. Tell the table which you mean before you filter, sort or total.',
  result: `<p><strong>What you will end with:</strong> the Received column read as day-first dates, so a 1 to 15 November range matches 4 samples, and the Weight column read with comma decimals, so its total is 2,271.25 g from 8 readable cells instead of 3.25 g from 2.</p>`,
  body: `<h2>How the app detects types</h2><ul><li><strong>Number</strong> when every non-blank cell reads as a dot-decimal number. Thousands separators must group three digits; $, €, £ and % are allowed.</li><li><strong>Date</strong> when every non-blank cell is a YYYY-MM-DD date. Native Confluence date chips read as YYYY-MM-DD.</li><li><strong>Text</strong> otherwise. Day-first or month-first text dates and comma decimals are never detected automatically.</li></ul><p>In the sample, Received and Weight (g) are detected as Text, and Cost as Number.</p>${sampleBlock('mixed-formats.csv', 'Sample measurements', { preview: 10 })}
<h2>Dates: choose the format before filtering</h2><ol><li>Choose <strong>Filters</strong>, then Received.</li><li>Set <strong>Treat column as</strong> to Date and <strong>Date format in table</strong> to <strong>DD/MM/YYYY</strong>.</li><li>Set <strong>Condition</strong> to <strong>Between</strong>, then enter <code>2026-11-01</code> and <code>2026-11-15</code> in <strong>From (inclusive)</strong> and <strong>To (inclusive)</strong>. Values can be typed as YYYY-MM-DD or in the selected format.</li></ol><p>The same range gives different answers depending on the format you choose:</p>${table(['Date format in table', 'Samples between 1 and 15 November'], [['YYYY-MM-DD', ex.T10.rangeIso.join(', ')], ['DD/MM/YYYY', ex.T10.rangeDmy.join(', ')], ['MM/DD/YYYY', ex.T10.rangeMdy.join(', ')]], { caption: 'Received Between 2026-11-01 and 2026-11-15' })}${table(['Sample', 'Received', 'Read as DD/MM/YYYY', 'Read as MM/DD/YYYY'], [['S-01', '2026-11-03', '3 Nov 2026', '3 Nov 2026'], ['S-02', '09/11/2026', '9 Nov 2026', '11 Sep 2026'], ['S-03', '28/10/2026', '28 Oct 2026', 'Not a date'], ['S-04', '11/12/2026', '11 Dec 2026', '12 Nov 2026'], ['S-05', '2026-11-14', '14 Nov 2026', '14 Nov 2026'], ['S-06', '', '(blank)', '(blank)'], ['S-07', 'TBC', 'Not a date', 'Not a date'], ['S-08', '31/11/2026', 'Not a date', 'Not a date'], ['S-09', '05/11/2026', '5 Nov 2026', '11 May 2026'], ['S-10', '2026-10-30', '30 Oct 2026', '30 Oct 2026']], { caption: 'How each Received value is read' })}<p>YYYY-MM-DD values read the same under every setting. Cells that are not valid dates, such as TBC or 31 November, never match a date condition.</p><h3>Sorting</h3><p>Sort Received ascending after setting DD/MM/YYYY: ${ex.T10.sortDmyAsc.join(', ')}. Valid dates come first in date order, then cells that are not dates, and blank cells stay at the bottom in either direction. Left as Text, the same sort compares characters: ${ex.T10.sortTextAsc.join(', ')}, which puts 5 November before 28 October.</p>
<h2>Numbers: choose dot or comma decimals</h2><p>Choose Weight (g) in <strong>Filters</strong>, set <strong>Treat column as</strong> to Number, and choose <strong>Number format in table and values</strong>. The format applies to cells and to the values you type, to sorting and to numeric summaries.</p>${table(['Weight (g) cell', '1,234.56 (dot decimal)', '1.234,56 (comma decimal)'], [['1,5', 'Unreadable', '1.5'], ['12,75', 'Unreadable', '12.75'], ['0,8', 'Unreadable', '0.8'], ['1.250', '1.25', '1250'], ['2', '2', '2'], ['', '(blank)', '(blank)'], ['n/a', 'Unreadable', 'Unreadable'], ['3,25', 'Unreadable', '3.25'], ['1.000,5', 'Unreadable', '1000.5'], ['0,45', 'Unreadable', '0.45']], { caption: 'How each Weight (g) value is read' })}<p>With comma decimals, Weight <strong>At least</strong> <code>1</code> matches ${ex.T10.weightAtLeastOneComma.length} samples (${ex.T10.weightAtLeastOneComma.join(', ')}); with dot decimals only ${ex.T10.weightAtLeastOneDot.join(' and ')} match. A tag above the table, <strong>Comma decimals: “Weight (g)”</strong>, reminds readers which format is active. Type filter values in the same format: with comma decimals, enter <code>1,5</code>, not <code>1.5</code>.</p><h3>Totals</h3><p>Choose <strong>Summary</strong>, set <strong>Summarize column</strong> to Weight (g) and <strong>Summary number format</strong> to 1.234,56 (comma decimal):</p>${table(['Summary number format', 'Sum', 'Cells counted'], [['1.234,56 (comma decimal)', '2,271.25', '8 numeric · 1 blank · 1 unreadable'], ['1,234.56 (dot decimal)', '3.25', '2 numeric · 1 blank · 7 unreadable']], { caption: 'Weight (g) summary for all 10 rows', numeric: ['Sum'] })}<p>Totals display in your browser’s number format. Blank and unreadable cells are excluded, and the counts show how many were left out, so an unexpectedly small total is easy to spot. The Cost column, detected as Number, totals 3,514.24 from 9 cells.</p>
<h2>Relative dates use the device calendar</h2><p>Relative presets compare against a reference date shown under the condition, for example <strong>Reference date: 2026-11-09 (your device’s local calendar).</strong> On that date:</p>${table(['Preset', 'Matches dates from', 'To'], [['Today', '2026-11-09', '2026-11-09'], ['Yesterday', '2026-11-08', '2026-11-08'], ['Tomorrow', '2026-11-10', '2026-11-10'], ['Last 7 days', '2026-11-03', '2026-11-09'], ['Next 7 days', '2026-11-09', '2026-11-15'], ['This week', '2026-11-09', '2026-11-15'], ['This month', '2026-11-01', '2026-11-30']], { caption: 'Relative date presets on Monday 9 November 2026' })}<p>Last and next day windows include today, weeks run Monday through Sunday, and <strong>Last N days</strong> and <strong>Next N days</strong> accept 1 to 3650. Readers in different time zones can be on different calendar days.</p>
<h2>Messages you may see</h2><p>Invalid values appear under <strong>Check your filter values</strong>, and that condition matches no rows until corrected:</p><ul><li><strong>Enter a valid number.</strong> For example, <code>1,5</code> in a dot-decimal column.</li><li><strong>Enter a valid date as YYYY-MM-DD or in the selected date format.</strong></li><li><strong>The lower bound must not exceed the upper bound.</strong></li></ul>
<h2>Publish the right formats</h2><p>An author can store number formats for every reader in <strong>Default numeric columns</strong>, one exact header per line, such as <code>Weight (g) = comma</code>. Date formats are chosen per default filter in its <strong>date format in table and values</strong> setting. The most reliable fix is in the source: use native date chips or YYYY-MM-DD, and one decimal style per column.</p>
${related(['T16', 'T11', 'T05', 'T03'])}`,
});

