// T12
ex.T12 = { matching: 125, total: 150, pages: 5, formulaCells: 18 };
pages.T12 = render('T12', {
  description: 'Download the filtered result or the original Confluence table as CSV or XLSX with Flint Table Filter, and check rows, columns and leading-zero IDs.',
  lede: 'Hand a table to a spreadsheet, a finance system or a colleague. Decide whether you need what you are looking at or the whole original table, download it, then check the file before you rely on it.',
  result: `<p><strong>What you will end with:</strong> from 150 orders, a current-result file with the 125 orders that are not cancelled, all of them rather than one page, in four columns in your chosen order. Alongside it, an original-table file with all 150 orders and six columns. Order IDs such as 000101 keep their leading zeros.</p>`,
  body: `<h2>Before you start</h2><p>The sample orders have six-digit IDs with leading zeros, customer names in several scripts, and notes that start with +, - or @, which spreadsheets can treat as formulas.</p>${sampleBlock('order-lines.csv', 'Sample orders', { full: false, note: 'If a spreadsheet app opens this file, import Order ID as text so its leading zeros survive.' })}${setupSample('order-lines.csv')}
<h2>Step 1: Shape the current result</h2><ol><li>Choose <strong>Filters</strong>, then Status. Set <strong>Condition</strong> to <strong>Is not one of</strong> and exclude Cancelled. The count line reads <strong>125 of 150 rows</strong>.</li><li>Set <strong>Rows per page</strong> to 25. The page controls read <strong>1–25 of 125 · Page 1 of 5</strong>. Only 25 rows are on screen, but the export includes all 125.</li><li>Choose <strong>Columns</strong> and clear Region and Note. Set <strong>Column to move</strong> to Status and choose <strong>Move earlier</strong> twice, so the visible order is Order ID, Status, Customer, Amount.</li><li>Sort Amount descending.</li></ol>
<h2>Step 2: Download both scopes</h2><ol><li>Choose <strong>Export</strong>. With <strong>Export scope</strong> set to <strong>Current result</strong>, the dialog explains: <strong>The CSV contains all matching rows in their current sort order, with visible columns in their current order.</strong></li><li>Choose <strong>Download CSV</strong> and <strong>Download XLSX</strong>. The files are named <code>flint-table-result.csv</code> and <code>flint-table-result.xlsx</code>.</li><li>Change <strong>Export scope</strong> to <strong>Original table</strong>. The dialog now reads <strong>The CSV contains all 150 source rows and all 6 columns from the first table inside this macro, in original order. Search, filters, group focus and column layout do not change this export.</strong> Download both formats again: <code>flint-table-original.csv</code> and <code>flint-table-original.xlsx</code>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Done</strong>.</li></ol>${files([['exports/orders/flint-table-result.csv', 'flint-table-result.csv', 'Current result: 125 data rows and a header, 4 columns, Amount descending'], ['exports/orders/flint-table-result.xlsx', 'flint-table-result.xlsx', 'The same current result, one worksheet named Table, every cell stored as text'], ['exports/orders/flint-table-original.csv', 'flint-table-original.csv', 'Original table: 150 data rows and a header, 6 columns, source order'], ['exports/orders/flint-table-original.xlsx', 'flint-table-original.xlsx', 'The same original table as XLSX']])}<p>These sample files were produced from the fictional table by the export logic of Flint Table Filter 2.5.0, running outside Confluence. They show the released file format; they are not downloads from a customer site.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Check the files</h2>${table(['Check', 'Current result', 'Original table'], [['Data rows', '125, all pages', '150'], ['Columns', '4: Order ID, Status, Customer, Amount', '6, in source order'], ['Row order', 'Amount, highest first', 'As in the source table'], ['Hidden columns', 'Left out', 'Included'], ['Formula-like notes', 'Not present (Note is hidden)', '18 CSV cells prefixed with an apostrophe']], { caption: 'What each download should contain' })}<p>The current-result CSV begins:</p>${code(`"Order ID","Status","Customer","Amount"
"000222","Processing","Nguyễn Thị Lan","2,406.99"
"000131","Shipped","Acme Fabrication","2,400.70"
"000161","Shipped","Acme Fabrication","2,376.40"`)}<p>The original-table CSV keeps every column, and its fourth order shows the formula protection:</p>${code(`"Order ID","Customer","Region","Status","Amount","Note"
"000101","Acme Fabrication","North America","Shipped","25.00","Gift wrap"
"000102","Nguyễn Thị Lan","Europe","Processing","104.19",""
"000103","Müller & Söhne","Asia Pacific","Processing","183.38","Leave at reception"
"000104","Ōtsuka Design","Latin America","Shipped","262.57","'+1 555 0142 callback"`)}<ul><li><strong>Row counts.</strong> Open the file and count data rows below the header: 125 and 150. If your spreadsheet shows 25, you are looking at a copy of one page, not this export.</li><li><strong>Leading zeros.</strong> Both formats contain 000222 exactly. A spreadsheet app can convert CSV values to numbers when it opens the file, dropping the zeros, unless you import the column as text. The XLSX stores every value as text, so 000222 stays.</li><li><strong>Formula-like values.</strong> In CSV, values that begin with =, +, - or @, including headers, are prefixed with an apostrophe, so <code>+1 555 0142 callback</code> becomes <code>'+1 555 0142 callback</code>. This changes the exported data and is not a guarantee of safety in every spreadsheet. The XLSX keeps the original text as a text cell.</li><li><strong>Text encoding.</strong> CSV files are UTF-8 with a byte order mark, so names such as Zoë Café and Nguyễn Thị Lan survive in apps that respect it.</li></ul><p>We opened both sample workbooks with an independent spreadsheet library: every cell is text, and every value matches the CSV once the apostrophe prefixes are taken into account.</p>
<h2>Group focus and grouped rows</h2><p>If you chose <strong>Show rows</strong> for a group, the current result contains only that group’s rows, and the dialog says so. With <strong>Group rich rows with subtotals</strong> on, data rows follow the group order, then the sort order within each group. Group headings and subtotals never appear in CSV or XLSX.</p>
<h2>Copy instead of downloading</h2><p>The dialog also includes <strong>CSV text</strong> for copying into another tool: select all in the field, copy, and save as a UTF-8 .csv file. Copying is limited to 500,000 characters. Downloads remain available for larger results.</p>
<h2>CSV and XLSX compared with Confluence PDF and Word</h2><p>CSV and XLSX contain extracted text: no colors, status label styling or links, only the text of each cell. Confluence’s own PDF and Word exports work differently. They include the complete original table, regardless of filters, hidden columns or any reader’s view, and Confluence controls their layout. Use CSV or XLSX when you need the filtered data; use PDF or Word when you need the page as written.</p>
<h2>If a download is unavailable</h2><ul><li><strong>Wait for the current view to finish updating before copying.</strong> A search was still being applied.</li><li><strong>Apply or discard your advanced rule changes before copying.</strong> An advanced rule draft is open.</li><li><strong>Correct the invalid filters before copying.</strong> A filter value is invalid; see ${guide('T10', 'number and date formats')}.</li><li><strong>This XLSX exceeds the 64 MB download limit. Use CSV for this table.</strong> XLSX also limits each cell to 32,767 characters. CSV downloads do not have these XLSX limits.</li></ul>
${related(['T03', 'T11', 'T13', 'T16'])}`,
});

// T13
ex.T13 = { total: 240, columns: 12, pages: { 25: 10, 50: 5, 100: 3 }, openP1: 20 };
pages.T13 = render('T13', {
  description: 'Hide, reorder, resize and pin columns, use sticky headers and row density, expand the table and choose rows per page for large Confluence tables.',
  lede: 'A 12-column, 240-row case log is hard to read in a page column. Shape the view you need, in your browser, without changing the stored table.',
  result: `<p><strong>What you will end with:</strong> a triage layout with 6 of 12 columns in a useful order, Case ID pinned while you scroll sideways, compact rows under a sticky header, 25 rows per page, and a full-screen view when you need more room, saved for reuse.</p>`,
  body: `<h2>Before you start</h2><p>The sample is a fictional support case log. It is large enough to need paging and horizontal scrolling, but it is a repeatable example, not a performance limit: how fast very large tables respond depends on the reader’s browser and device.</p>${sampleBlock('support-case-log.csv', 'Sample case log', { preview: 3, full: false })}${setupSample('support-case-log.csv')}
<h2>Step 1: Keep the columns you read</h2><ol><li>Choose <strong>Columns</strong>. Under <strong>Visible columns</strong>, clear Opened, Customer, Region, Assignee, Channel and Last update. The button reads <strong>Columns (6/12)</strong>. At least one column must stay visible.</li><li>Use <strong>Column to move</strong> with <strong>Move earlier</strong> and <strong>Move later</strong> until the visible order is Case ID, Priority, Status, SLA due, Product, Summary. Hidden columns keep their place in the list; <strong>Restore column order</strong> undoes every move.</li></ol><p>Hidden columns still take part in search, filters and sorting, so a search for a customer name still finds its cases.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Set column widths</h2><ol><li>Under <strong>Column widths</strong>, set <strong>Width for column</strong> to Summary and <strong>Column width</strong> to <strong>Wide (320 px)</strong>.</li><li>Set Case ID to <strong>Compact (120 px)</strong>.</li><li>For another size, choose <strong>Custom</strong>, enter a whole number from 80 to 1000 in <strong>Width in pixels</strong> and choose <strong>Apply width</strong>.</li></ol><p><strong>Original</strong> restores one column; <strong>Restore all original widths</strong> restores them all. Wide columns may need horizontal scrolling.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Density, sticky header and pinning</h2><p>Open <strong>Saved views and presentation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Row density</strong>: Compact fits more rows on screen; Spacious suits short tables read aloud in meetings. Comfortable is the default.</li><li><strong>Sticky header</strong> is on by default and keeps column names visible while you scroll down the table.</li><li><strong>Pin columns while scrolling horizontally</strong>: select Case ID. Pinned columns stay at the left edge while the rest of the table scrolls sideways.</li></ul>
<h2>Step 4: Page size and full screen</h2><p>Search, filters and sorting apply to all 240 rows before paging. Choose the page size that suits the task:</p>${table(['Rows per page', 'Pages for 240 rows'], [['25', '10'], ['50 (default)', '5'], ['100', '3']], { caption: 'Pages for the full case log', numeric: ['Pages for 240 rows'] })}<p>Use <strong>Previous</strong>, <strong>Next</strong> or <strong>Go to page</strong> to move between pages. Filtering shortens the list: Priority <strong>Is one of</strong> P1 with Status <strong>Is not one of</strong> Resolved and Closed leaves 20 cases on a single page.</p><p>Choose <strong>Expand table</strong> to read in a full-screen <strong>Expanded table</strong> dialog. It keeps your columns, widths, pins and filters, and offers page size buttons and page controls at the bottom. Choose <strong>Close</strong> or press Escape to return.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Save the layout</h2><p>Name the layout <code>Triage layout</code> in <strong>View name</strong> and choose <strong>Save personal view</strong>. It keeps columns, order, widths, density, sticky header, pins and page size in this browser. See ${guide('T07', 'personal views')}.</p>
<h2>What authors can set for everyone</h2><p>Column visibility, order, widths and page size can be author defaults. Density, sticky header and pinning are reader presentation settings; publish them as a ${guide('T08', 'shared view')}.</p>${settings([['Default hidden columns', 'Opened<br>Customer<br>Region<br>Assignee<br>Channel<br>Last update'], ['Default column order', 'Case ID<br>Priority<br>Status<br>SLA due'], ['Default column widths', 'Summary = 320<br>Case ID = 120'], ['Default rows per page', '25']])}<p>Columns not listed in <strong>Default column order</strong> follow in their original order. Each name must match a header exactly.</p>
<h2>What to know</h2><ul><li>Hiding columns changes presentation only. It does not restrict access; the data stays in the page and in Confluence PDF and Word exports.</li><li><strong>Current result</strong> downloads leave hidden columns out; <strong>Original table</strong> downloads include them. See ${guide('T12', 'CSV and Excel exports')}.</li><li>Your layout never changes the stored table or other readers’ views.</li></ul>
${related(['T07', 'T08', 'T04', 'T12'])}`,
});

// T14
ex.T14 = {
  logical: [['Week 45', 'Mon', 'Ana', 'Ben'], ['Week 45', 'Tue', 'Ana', 'Chen'], ['Week 45', 'Wed', 'Dev', 'Ben'], ['Week 46', 'Mon', 'Chen', 'Ana'], ['Week 46', 'Tue', 'Dev', 'Ana'], ['Week 47', 'Office holiday', 'Office holiday', 'Office holiday']],
  anaPrimary: [['Week 45', 'Mon', 'Ana', 'Ben'], ['Week 45', 'Tue', 'Ana', 'Chen']],
  weeks: [['Week 45', 3], ['Week 46', 2], ['Week 47', 1]],
};
const rosterHtml = `<div class="guide-table"><table><caption>Table 2 as it appears in the page</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Week</th><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Primary</th><th scope="col">Backup</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="3">Week 45</td><td>Mon</td><td>Ana</td><td>Ben</td></tr><tr><td>Tue</td><td>Ana</td><td>Chen</td></tr><tr><td>Wed</td><td>Dev</td><td>Ben</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="2">Week 46</td><td>Mon</td><td>Chen</td><td>Ana</td></tr><tr><td>Tue</td><td>Dev</td><td>Ana</td></tr><tr><td>Week 47</td><td colspan="3">Office holiday</td></tr></tbody></table></div>`;
pages.T14 = render('T14', {
  description: 'Point Flint Table Filter at the right native table in a macro, choose a header mode, and see how merged cells expand into rows you can filter and total.',
  lede: 'The macro filters one native table at a time. When its body holds a legend and a roster, a first row without header formatting, or merged cells, these settings decide what readers can filter.',
  result: `<p><strong>What you will end with:</strong> the second table in the macro selected as the source, its header detected automatically, and its merged Week cells expanded into 6 rows, so Primary <strong>Is one of</strong> Ana returns 2 rows, both labeled Week 45.</p>`,
  body: `<h2>The example</h2><p>An on-call page holds a legend table, a short paragraph and a roster inside one Flint Table Filter macro. The roster merges each week’s cell down its days and spans a holiday across three columns.</p>${table(['Code', 'Meaning'], [['P', 'Primary on-call'], ['B', 'Backup on-call'], ['H', 'Office holiday']], { caption: 'Table 1, the legend' })}${rosterHtml}
<h2>Choose the source table</h2><ol><li>Edit the page and open the macro settings. <strong>Settings section</strong> starts at <strong>Source and starting view</strong>.</li><li>In <strong>Source table (position inside this macro, starting at 1)</strong>, enter <code>2</code>. Blank or 1 selects the first table.</li><li><strong>Starting view preview</strong> switches to the roster and warns that merged cells repeat covered values. Choose <strong>Save settings</strong> and publish.</li></ol><p>Positions count the native tables placed directly in the macro body, in order. Tables inside other elements, such as an expand, are not counted and cannot be selected. Readers see <strong>Controls apply to native table 2. Choose another source table in the macro settings; other tables keep their original content.</strong> The legend stays on the page unchanged.</p><p>If the position does not exist, readers see <strong>Selected table is unavailable</strong> with the complete source below it. Correct the number in the macro settings.</p>
<h2>Choose a header mode</h2><p><strong>Header row</strong> offers <strong>Automatic (Confluence header cells)</strong>, <strong>First row is header</strong> and <strong>No header row</strong>. Automatic uses the first row only when it is formatted as Confluence header cells.</p>${table(['Header row setting', 'First row uses header cells', 'First row uses ordinary cells'], [['Automatic (Confluence header cells)', 'Columns Week, Day, Primary, Backup; 2 data rows', 'Columns Column 1 to Column 4; 3 data rows'], ['First row is header', 'Columns Week, Day, Primary, Backup; 2 data rows', 'Columns Week, Day, Primary, Backup; 2 data rows'], ['No header row', 'Columns Column 1 to Column 4; 3 data rows', 'Columns Column 1 to Column 4; 3 data rows']], { caption: 'A two-day table with a Week, Day, Primary, Backup first row' })}<p>A notice under the table states the mode in use: <strong>The first row is used as the header. Change Header row in the macro settings to use another mode.</strong>, <strong>No header row selected. All rows are included; filters use numbered column names.</strong> or <strong>No header row detected. All rows are included; filters use numbered column names.</strong> Settings that name headers, such as a default sort or default filter, need a header row; without one, readers see a <strong>Check the table's default view</strong> warning.</p>
<h2>How merged cells become rows</h2><p>For filtering, sorting, totals and downloads, each merged cell is repeated in every row and column it covers. The roster becomes six logical rows:</p>${table(['Week', 'Day', 'Primary', 'Backup'], ex.T14.logical, { caption: 'The roster as the app reads it' })}<p>Now each row answers questions on its own:</p><ul><li>Primary <strong>Is one of</strong> Ana returns ${ex.T14.anaPrimary.length} rows, Week 45 Mon and Week 45 Tue. The week label comes with each row, even though only the first row holds it in the page.</li><li>Searching <code>week 45</code> returns 3 rows.</li><li>Grouping by Week counts ${ex.T14.weeks.map(([week, count]) => `${week}: ${count}`).join(', ')}.</li><li>The holiday cell spans Day, Primary and Backup, so Primary’s values include Office holiday.</li></ul><p>Readers see <strong>Merged cells repeat their rich content in each covered row and column for filtering, sorting, totals and CSV/XLSX. Totals count repeated values. The saved source and Confluence PDF/Word exports retain the original merges.</strong> If a merged cell holds a number, it is counted once for every row it covers; ${guide('T16', 'troubleshooting')} shows an example.</p>
<h2>When merged cells cannot be expanded</h2><p>Overlapping or invalid spans, or a table too large to expand safely (more than 10,000 rows, more than 1,000 columns or more than 100,000 cells after expansion), show <strong>Filtering is unavailable</strong> with the reason, for example <strong>The merged table has invalid spans or exceeds the safe logical-grid size.</strong> The original table is shown complete. Unmerge the cells or split the table to restore filtering.</p>
<h2>Sources this release does not filter</h2><p>Flint Table Filter works with native Confluence tables placed directly inside the macro. Tables produced by other macros or apps, embedded spreadsheets, page properties reports and excerpts are outside this release, as are pivots, charts, formulas, joins, imports and editing.</p>
${related(['T16', 'T11', 'T10', 'T01'])}`,
});

// T15
ex.T15 = {
  highlights: { Severity: ['R-02', 'R-07'], Score: ['R-01', 'R-02', 'R-07', 'R-11', 'R-14'], Owner: ['R-04', 'R-09', 'R-14', 'R-20'], Status: ['R-03', 'R-08', 'R-15', 'R-20'] },
  overlapWarning: ['R-01', 'R-04', 'R-05', 'R-06', 'R-11', 'R-13', 'R-14', 'R-15', 'R-18'],
  overlapNegative: ['R-02', 'R-07'],
};
pages.T15 = render('T15', {
  description: 'Add conditional background colors to Confluence table cells with Flint Table Filter, keep the meaning readable as text, and share the highlighting as a view.',
  lede: 'Filtering removes what you do not need; highlighting marks what matters in the rows you keep. Color the cells that need attention while the whole register stays in view.',
  result: `<p><strong>What you will end with:</strong> all 20 entries of a risk register still visible, with the 2 Critical severity cells marked Negative, the 5 scores above 15 marked Warning, the 4 empty Owner cells marked Information and the 4 Closed status cells marked Positive, shared with the team as a view.</p>`,
  body: `<h2>What conditional formatting colors</h2><p>Each rule tests one column and colors that column’s cell when the condition matches. It does not color the whole row. Use several rules to mark several columns.</p>${sampleBlock('risk-register.csv', 'Sample register', { preview: 4, note: `This is the register from ${guide('T02', 'the risk review guide')}.` })}
<h2>Step 1: Add the rules</h2><ol><li>Open <strong>Saved views and presentation</strong>, then <strong>Conditional formatting</strong>.</li><li>Set <strong>Format column</strong> to Severity, <strong>Condition</strong> to <strong>Equals text</strong>, <strong>Comparison value</strong> to <code>Critical</code> and <strong>Appearance</strong> to <strong>Negative</strong>. Choose <strong>Add formatting rule</strong>.</li><li>Add three more rules the same way:</li></ol>${table(['Format column', 'Condition', 'Comparison value', 'Appearance'], [['Severity', 'Equals text', 'Critical', 'Negative'], ['Score', 'Greater than', '15', 'Warning'], ['Owner', 'Is empty', 'None needed', 'Information'], ['Status', 'Equals text', 'Closed', 'Positive']], { caption: 'Four formatting rules' })}<p>Each rule appears in a list with a <strong>Remove</strong> button. Above the table, a line describes the active rules in words: <strong>Conditional formatting: Severity equals Critical (negative); Score greater than 15 (warning); Owner is empty (information); Status equals Closed (positive)</strong>.</p>
<h2>Expected highlighting</h2>${table(['Column', 'Appearance', 'Highlighted entries'], [['Severity', 'Negative', ex.T15.highlights.Severity.join(', ')], ['Score', 'Warning', ex.T15.highlights.Score.join(', ')], ['Owner', 'Information', ex.T15.highlights.Owner.join(', ')], ['Status', 'Positive', ex.T15.highlights.Status.join(', ')]], { caption: 'Highlighted cells across all 20 entries' })}<p>Greater than means strictly greater: R-05 and R-06 score exactly 15 and stay uncolored. R-14 gets two highlights, one for its score and one for its missing owner.</p>
<h2>Highlighting is not filtering</h2><p>The count line still reads <strong>20 of 20 rows</strong>. Highlighted rows remain part of the result, and summaries, group totals and downloads ignore color entirely; CSV and XLSX files contain no formatting. Combine the two when you want both: exclude Closed in <strong>Filters</strong> and 16 entries remain, still highlighted.</p>
<h2>When rules overlap</h2><p>Rules apply in the order you add them, and when two rules match the same cell, the later rule’s color wins. To show a two-level scale on Score, add the broad rule first and the strict rule second:</p><ol><li>Score, <strong>Greater than</strong>, <code>10</code>, Warning</li><li>Score, <strong>Greater than</strong>, <code>18</code>, Negative</li></ol><p>R-02 (20) and R-07 (25) show Negative; ${ex.T15.overlapWarning.length} entries from 12 to 16 show Warning. Added in the opposite order, the Warning rule would cover the Negative one.</p>
<h2>Keep it readable</h2><ul><li>Color should never be the only signal. Keep the meaning in the cell text, such as Critical or Closed, and rely on the rule description line for readers who cannot distinguish the backgrounds.</li><li>The four appearances use Confluence table cell background colors. Check contrast in the light or dark theme your readers use.</li><li>Prefer a few rules with distinct meanings over many shades. A view can hold up to ten rules.</li></ul>
<h2>Share the highlighting</h2><p>Conditional formatting is part of a reader’s view. It is not an author default, and <strong>Reset view</strong> clears it. To keep it, save a ${guide('T07', 'personal view')}. To give it to the team, prepare a ${guide('T08', 'shared view')}: readers choose it in <strong>Reader view</strong> and choose <strong>Apply view</strong>. The page always opens without highlighting until a view is applied.</p>
<h2>Rule types and messages</h2><ul><li><strong>Contains text</strong> and <strong>Equals text</strong> ignore case. <strong>Greater than</strong> and <strong>Less than</strong> compare numbers using the column’s number format. <strong>Is empty</strong> and <strong>Is not empty</strong> need no value.</li><li><strong>Enter a value for this formatting rule.</strong> appears if the comparison value is missing, and <strong>Enter a valid number.</strong> if a number rule has text such as “high”.</li><li><strong>Use at most ten conditional formatting rules.</strong> appears when you try to add an eleventh.</li></ul>
${related(['T02', 'T08', 'T07', 'T13'])}`,
});

// T16
ex.T16 = { mergedRows: [['Design review', 'Web', '5'], ['Copy review', 'Web', '5'], ['Load test', 'Platform', '3']], mergedSum: 13 };
pages.T16 = render('T16', {
  description: 'Fix Flint Table Filter problems: missing controls, license notices, empty results, invalid values, stale saved views, group focus and merged-cell counts.',
  lede: 'Most problems come from the table source, a filter value, or a saved setting that no longer matches the table. Start with the message you see, then work through the checks below.',
  result: `<p><strong>Quick checks:</strong> read the count line under the controls (for example <strong>12 of 24 rows</strong>), the tags above the table, any <strong>Author defaults currently applied</strong> line, any group focus banner and the notices under the table. Together they explain almost every unexpected result.</p>`,
  body: `<h2 id="no-controls">The table shows but the controls do not</h2>${table(['What you see', 'Why', 'What to do'], [['An active license is needed to filter this table', 'The app has no active subscription or trial on this site. The complete table stays visible.', 'Ask your Confluence administrator to start a trial or renew the subscription.'], ['Add a table inside this macro', 'The macro body has no native table. Tables produced by other macros or apps and embedded spreadsheets do not count.', 'Edit the page and add or paste a native Confluence table inside the macro.'], ['Selected table is unavailable', '<strong>Source table</strong> points to a position with no table.', 'Enter an existing position, starting at 1, in the macro settings.'], ['Filtering is unavailable', 'Merged cells overlap, are invalid or would expand beyond safe limits.', 'Unmerge the cells or split the table. The original table remains visible.'], ['The table is taking longer to load', 'The table did not arrive from Confluence in time.', 'Refresh the page. If it persists, contact your Confluence administrator.'], ['No controls and no notice', 'The table sits outside the Flint Table Filter macro.', 'Move the table into the macro body, or insert the macro and place the table inside it.']], { caption: 'Messages in place of the controls' })}<p>Check the published page. While editing, <strong>Starting view preview</strong> in the macro settings shows what readers will get.</p>
<h2 id="no-rows">No rows match</h2><p>An empty result shows <strong>No matching rows</strong> with <strong>Clear search</strong> and <strong>Reset view</strong>. Work through these causes:</p><ul><li><strong>A filter combination.</strong> Filters on different columns must all match. Read the tags above the table and remove one at a time. To ask “this or that” across columns, use ${guide('T09', 'advanced groups')}.</li><li><strong>An invalid value.</strong> <strong>Check your filter values</strong> lists it, for example <strong>Due: Enter a valid date as YYYY-MM-DD or in the selected date format.</strong> for <code>11/09/2026</code> in a YYYY-MM-DD column. That condition matches nothing until corrected; see ${guide('T10', 'number and date formats')}.</li><li><strong>An invalid advanced rule.</strong> <strong>Check your advanced filters</strong> names the group and rule. The whole expression matches nothing until it is fixed.</li><li><strong>A relative date.</strong> Presets follow your device’s calendar. The reference date is shown under the condition.</li><li><strong>An exact value.</strong> <strong>Is one of</strong> compares the full cell text, including capitalization and extra words. Use <strong>Contains</strong> for partial text.</li><li><strong>Group focus.</strong> <strong>No matching rows in the focused group</strong> means the focus is still active. Choose <strong>Clear group focus</strong>.</li></ul>
<h2 id="counts">The count or total is not what you expected</h2><ul><li><strong>Group focus is active.</strong> The count line reads, for example, <strong>16 focused rows · 48 matching before group focus · 64 total rows</strong>, and a banner names the focused group.</li><li><strong>Author defaults apply.</strong> The page may open filtered. The line <strong>Author defaults currently applied</strong> lists them, and <strong>Reset view</strong> returns to them rather than to the unfiltered table.</li><li><strong>The header row is read as data.</strong> If the notice says <strong>No header row detected</strong>, the first row counts as a data row. Format it as header cells or set <strong>Header row</strong> to <strong>First row is header</strong>.</li><li><strong>A search matched a hidden column.</strong> Search covers every column, visible or not.</li><li><strong>Some cells are not numbers.</strong> Summaries list numeric, blank and unreadable cells; only numeric cells are totaled.</li><li><strong>Merged cells repeat values.</strong> Each covered position gets a copy, as in the example below.</li></ul>${mergedTotalExample()}
<h2 id="views">A saved view is missing or will not apply</h2><ul><li><strong>Personal views live in one browser.</strong> They do not appear in another browser, device, profile or private window, and clearing site data removes them. <strong>Personal view storage is unavailable for this browser session.</strong> means the browser blocks storage.</li><li><strong>The table layout changed.</strong> Renaming, adding, removing or reordering header cells, changing <strong>Header row</strong>, or replacing the table means personal views saved for the old layout no longer appear. Shared views report <strong>These views belong to a different table or header layout. Save them again for this table.</strong></li><li><strong>Something is still pending.</strong> Saving and applying wait for the search to finish, for any advanced rule draft to be applied or discarded, and for invalid filters to be corrected. The status line says which.</li><li><strong>The view no longer fits the table.</strong> <strong>Reader view could not be applied</strong> appears above the table with the reason. Rebuild and save the view.</li></ul><p>See ${guide('T07', 'personal views')} and ${guide('T08', 'shared views')}.</p>
<h2 id="defaults">The starting view shows a warning</h2><p><strong>Check the table's default view</strong> lists each author setting that was skipped and why. For example, after Due is renamed: <strong>The saved default sort was skipped because its header name is missing or blank. Update the macro settings to use a current, unique header name.</strong> Copied advanced filters are skipped as a whole rather than partly applied. Update the header names in the macro settings, or copy the advanced settings again, then publish.</p>
<h2 id="rich-content">Rich content or links look wrong</h2><p>Choose <strong>Show native rich content</strong> to display the table with Confluence’s own rendering, then <strong>Show interactive table</strong> to return. If a link reports <strong>The link could not open. Use native rich content to follow it.</strong>, follow it from the native view.</p>
<h2 id="exports">A download differs from the screen</h2><p><strong>Current result</strong> follows your filters, group focus, visible columns and sort; <strong>Original table</strong> ignores them. CSV prefixes formula-like values with an apostrophe. Confluence PDF and Word exports always contain the complete original table. See ${guide('T12', 'CSV and Excel exports')}.</p>
<h2 id="reset">Start again</h2><p><strong>Reset view</strong> restores the author’s starting view and keeps your saved views. Choosing <strong>Author default</strong> in <strong>Reader view</strong> and <strong>Apply view</strong> does the same. A reload also clears unsaved changes.</p>
<h2 id="support">Contact support</h2><p>If none of this explains the problem, <a href="${ROOT}support/">contact support</a> with the exact message, what you expected, your browser, and a small example table with private information removed. Please do not send confidential page content.</p>
${related(['T14', 'T10', 'T07', 'T12'], [`The product guide’s <a href="${ROOT}table-filter/#setup">setup section</a> covers macro settings, source table and header choices.`])}`,
});

function mergedTotalExample() {
  const head = '<thead><tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Team</th><th scope="col">Estimate</th></tr></thead>';
  const page = `<div class="guide-table"><table><caption>A three-task table with Team and Estimate merged across two rows</caption>${head}<tbody><tr><td>Design review</td><td rowspan="2">Web</td><td class="num" rowspan="2">5</td></tr><tr><td>Copy review</td></tr><tr><td>Load test</td><td>Platform</td><td class="num">3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>`;
  return `${page}${table(['Task', 'Team', 'Estimate'], ex.T16.mergedRows, { caption: 'The same table as the app reads it', numeric: ['Estimate'] })}<p>The page shows estimates of 5 and 3, but the app reads three rows, and the Estimate summary totals ${ex.T16.mergedSum}: the merged 5 is counted once for each task it covers. If that is not what you mean, unmerge the cell and enter the estimate where it belongs.</p>`;
}

